# Parallel Agents for Large Changes

When in plan mode and the scope involves 7+ files or multiple independent concerns, **always** decompose the work into parallel agent tasks before writing any code.

## Step 1 — Decompose

Identify independent work units. Each unit must satisfy:
- Owns a disjoint set of files/directories — no file appears in two units
- Has no runtime dependency on another unit's output during the same execution wave
- Maps to a single layer or feature boundary (data, API, UI, navigation, config)

Split axes to use: feature boundary · architectural layer · directory ownership

## Step 2 — Write Precise Per-Agent Instructions

For **each** agent, produce a block with all six fields:

```
### Agent <letter> — <one-line purpose>

Goal:        <single sentence stating what this agent must achieve>
Owns:        <exhaustive list of files and directories this agent may touch>
Must not touch: <files owned by other agents that might be tempting>
Inputs:      <interfaces, types, or constants it may consume from other agents (read-only)>
Outputs:     <exported symbols, API routes, or UI components other agents depend on>
Acceptance:  <concrete, verifiable done-condition — e.g. "GET /api/fly/apps returns {apps:[]}">
```

Every field is required. Vague goals ("implement the feature") are not acceptable.

## Step 3 — Sequence Waves if Needed

If any agent depends on an output from another, split into waves:

```
Wave 1 (parallel): Agent A, Agent B   — produce shared types/interfaces
Wave 2 (parallel): Agent C, Agent D   — consume Wave 1 outputs, build logic
Wave 3 (parallel): Agent E            — wires navigation, depends on Wave 2 routes
```

Agents within a wave run concurrently. Never start a wave until the previous wave is complete.

## Step 4 — Present and Confirm

Show the full plan (all agents with their six-field blocks + wave structure) to the user before launching. Do not begin implementation until the user approves.

## Example

```
Large task: "Add Fly.io secrets management"

### Agent A — Data + API layer

Goal:        Implement Fly CLI wrapper and Next.js API routes for apps and secrets
Owns:        lib/fly/cli.ts, lib/fly/types.ts, app/api/fly/apps/route.ts,
             app/api/fly/apps/[app]/secrets/route.ts
Must not touch: components/, app/fly/, lib/nav/
Inputs:      none
Outputs:     FlyApp, FlySecret types; GET /api/fly/apps; GET|POST /api/fly/apps/[app]/secrets
Acceptance:  All four files exist; API routes return typed JSON without runtime errors

### Agent B — UI layer

Goal:        Build the Fly overview page and secrets bulk-edit panel
Owns:        app/fly/overview/page.tsx, app/fly/secrets/page.tsx,
             components/fly/fly-secrets-bulk-panel.tsx
Must not touch: lib/fly/, app/api/, lib/nav/, components/layout/
Inputs:      FlyApp, FlySecret types from Agent A (read-only import)
Outputs:     /fly/overview and /fly/secrets pages renderable in the shell
Acceptance:  Both pages compile; secrets panel lists and saves secrets via Agent A routes

### Agent C — Navigation wiring

Goal:        Add Fly entries to sidebar and topbar navigation
Owns:        lib/nav/aws-workspace-topbar.ts, components/layout/sidebar.tsx
Must not touch: lib/fly/, app/fly/, app/api/fly/, components/fly/
Inputs:      Route paths /fly/overview and /fly/secrets from Agent B (string constants only)
Outputs:     Sidebar group "Fly" with Overview and Secrets links visible in the shell
Acceptance:  Sidebar renders Fly group; active link highlights on /fly/* routes

Wave structure: Agent A and Agent B run in parallel (Wave 1).
               Agent C runs after Wave 1 is complete (Wave 2).
```

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Omitting any of the six per-agent fields
- ❌ Two agents with overlapping `Owns` lists
- ❌ Starting Wave 2 before Wave 1 acceptance criteria are verified
- ❌ One giant agent touching 10+ files sequentially
- ❌ Launching agents before the user has reviewed and approved the plan
